00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:41.920
In 1909, a man named Charles Hercules Ebbets began secretly buying up adjacent parcels of land

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:47.800
in the Flatbush section of Brooklyn, including the site of a garbage dump called Pig Town

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:53.860
because of the pigs that once ate their fill there and the stench that still filled the air.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:59.970
He hoped eventually to build a permanent home for the lackluster baseball team he had

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:01.880
once worked for and now owned.

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:05.820
The team was called the Trolley Dodgers,

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:09.530
or just the Dodgers, after the way their devoted fans

00:03:09.530 --> 00:03:11.790
negotiated Brooklyn's busy streets.

00:03:13.490 --> 00:03:16.350
In 1912, construction began.

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:19.470
By the time it was completed,

00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:23.070
Pig Town had been transformed into Ebbets Field,

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:27.690
baseball's newest shrine where some of the game's

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:29.310
greatest drama would take place.

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:37.470
In the years to come, Dodger fans would see more bad times than good, but hardly care.

00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:46.540
Listen to the southern cadences of a pioneer broadcaster and witness firsthand baseball's finest moment

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:52.500
when a black man wearing the number 42 trotted out to first base.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.140
In 1955, after more than four decades of frustration,

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:07.500
Brooklyn would finally win a world championship,

00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:12.810
