00:26:17.260 --> 00:26:21.180
and codify new rules that would change the game forever.

00:26:23.940 --> 00:26:26.220
The infield would now be diamond-shaped.

00:26:27.320 --> 00:26:29.980
Foul lines were established, and the batter guy...

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:37.010
three missed swings before he was called out most important runners would now be

00:26:37.010 --> 00:26:44.530
tagged or thrown out not thrown at it was now a more challenging game faster

00:26:44.530 --> 00:26:50.360
paced American and to the Knickerbockers great delight quite distinct from

00:26:50.360 --> 00:26:58.350
cricket and rounder but there was precious little room to play the new

00:26:58.350 --> 00:26:59.930
game in the crowded streets of

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:05.230
lower Manhattan, and the Knickerbockers had to travel across the Hudson River to Hoboken, New

00:27:05.230 --> 00:27:14.620
Jersey, and a grassy area called the Elysian Fields. They crossed the Barkley Street Ferry

00:27:14.620 --> 00:27:21.420
in a body, like unto the pilgrims of yore, and marched up the country road on the Jersey side,

00:27:22.260 --> 00:27:28.730
prospecting here and there for suitable grounds, until they reached the Elysian Field, where they

00:27:28.730 --> 00:27:29.010
settled.

00:27:30.280 --> 00:27:34.680
Then they perfected their organization, calling it the Knickerbocker,

00:27:35.440 --> 00:27:41.500
which was the nucleus of the great American game of baseball, Seymour Church.

00:27:43.880 --> 00:27:47.280
Twice a week we went over to the Elysian Fields for practice.

00:27:48.680 --> 00:27:52.580
Once there, we were free from all restraint and throwing off our coats.

00:27:52.600 --> 00:27:55.600
We played until it was too dark to see any longer.

00:27:56.830 --> 00:27:59.890
I was a left-handed batter and sometimes used to hit the ball.

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:05.160
into the river. People began to take an interest in the game presently, and sometimes we had as

00:28:05.160 --> 00:28:13.460
many as a hundred spectators watching. By the following spring, the Knickerbockers were finally

00:28:13.460 --> 00:28:22.630
ready to take on another team. On June 19th, 1846, at the Elysian Fields, they played against a group

00:28:22.630 --> 00:28:29.970
of cricket players in the first real baseball game in history. The Knickerbockers.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:35.340
lost 23-1, but their game spread throughout the city.

00:28:38.770 --> 00:28:42.640
By the 1850s, New York was baseball man.

00:29:02.590 --> 00:29:06.470
There were teams of doctors, teams of teachers, teams of tradesmen.

00:29:07.860 --> 00:29:09.280
Shipbuilders formed clubs.

00:29:09.560 --> 00:29:15.120
So did firemen, bankers, teamsters, lawyers, even undertakers.

00:29:21.230 --> 00:29:24.430
Meanwhile, the Knickerbockers continued to refine their game.

00:29:26.200 --> 00:29:29.880
The winning team was the first to get 21 aces, or run.

00:29:30.090 --> 00:29:34.770
soon changed to whoever was ahead at the end of nine innings.

00:29:36.020 --> 00:29:40.140
They standardized the number of men who could play on a side at nine

00:29:40.140 --> 00:29:43.500
and set the bases 90 feet apart.

00:29:46.510 --> 00:29:49.490
That's so interesting that it would come out 90 feet,

00:29:50.520 --> 00:29:53.420
that somebody sat down, Mr. Cartwright or whoever,

00:29:53.600 --> 00:29:54.940
and said, hey, it ought to be 90 feet.

00:29:55.000 --> 00:29:56.240
It just sounded like a logical number.

00:29:57.220 --> 00:29:59.980
The fact of the matter is, in retrospect, if it were...

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:03.580
88 feet, the game would be very different.

00:30:04.100 --> 00:30:05.620
Think of the plays at first base.

00:30:05.960 --> 00:30:09.420
Think of the double plays that wouldn't be completed

00:30:09.420 --> 00:30:11.960
on an 88-foot first base.

00:30:12.270 --> 00:30:13.870
Gee, and second base.

00:30:14.050 --> 00:30:17.640
If it were 94 feet, we'd be throwing people out

00:30:17.640 --> 00:30:19.110
all over the place.

00:30:19.270 --> 00:30:21.330
Batting averages would drop remarkably.

00:30:21.790 --> 00:30:25.010
So if 90 feet was something somebody said,

00:30:25.090 --> 00:30:28.230
hey, that's a good number, that was a pick from heaven.

00:30:36.070 --> 00:30:41.700
Alexander Joy Cartwright left Manhattan and helped spread baseball westward,

00:30:42.700 --> 00:30:47.540
across the Rockies, onto the California Gold Rush, then all the way to Hawaii.

00:30:49.430 --> 00:30:55.660
There, he became a wealthy merchant, but he never entirely lost interest in the team he'd helped to form

00:30:55.660 --> 00:30:57.860
or the game he'd helped lay out.

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:10.930
Dear old Knickerbockers, I hope the club is still kept up and I shall someday meet again with them on the pleasant fields of Hoboken.

00:31:12.560 --> 00:31:17.160
Have in my possession the original ball with which we used to play on Murray Hill.

00:31:19.050 --> 00:31:25.630
Sometimes I have thought of sending it home, but I cannot bear to part with it.

00:31:26.550 --> 00:31:29.880
So linked in with cherished home memory.

00:31:30.030 --> 00:31:59.290
It is the American game. That's just what it is. And actually, it makes you mean. I'm 8 of 1. But I can feel like I'm 15 when I'm talking baseball. I'm watching baseball.

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:06.400
This is it. It does this. It can do this to any man. It brings you back.

00:32:15.580 --> 00:32:22.370
It may be truly said that the year of 1856 was the birth year of the evolution of baseball.

00:32:23.950 --> 00:32:29.980
It was then that we took note of the possibilities of the game and saw in it a lever which...

00:32:30.000 --> 00:32:36.000
could be advantageously used to lift up athletic sports into a desired popularity.

00:32:37.310 --> 00:32:37.970
Henry Chadwick.

00:32:40.830 --> 00:32:47.210
In 1856, a British-born music teacher and enthusiastic cricketer named Henry Chadwick

00:32:47.430 --> 00:32:53.220
saw the Knickerbockers play the New York Gothams and became an instant convert to baseball.

00:32:54.780 --> 00:32:59.980
Americans do not care to dawdle over a sleep-inspiring game all through the...

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:11.060
heat of a June or July day. What they do, they want to do in a hurry. In baseball, all is lightning.

00:33:12.580 --> 00:33:19.980
Thus, the reason for the American antipathy to cricket can readily be understood. Chadwick developed

00:33:19.980 --> 00:33:25.940
the box score, wrote and edited the most popular player's manual, and launched one of the first

00:33:25.940 --> 00:33:29.880
baseball columns, covering the Knickerbockers and their challenges.

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:35.240
the Gothams, Eagles, Empires, Excelsiors, and Atlantics

00:33:35.240 --> 00:33:38.210
in the pages of the New York Clipper.

00:33:40.210 --> 00:33:42.550
And he began keeping comparative statistics

00:33:43.010 --> 00:33:47.220
so that he could measure one player's performance against another.

00:33:49.220 --> 00:33:53.600
Because we've been playing it fundamentally the same way for so long,

00:33:53.840 --> 00:33:55.980
the way that we can find the benchmarks

00:33:55.980 --> 00:33:58.500
that cross generations and cross decades

00:33:58.500 --> 00:33:59.980
is to be able to...

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:05.040
use these statistics as if they were labels they aren't numbers 300 hitter

00:34:05.040 --> 00:34:10.470
is not a number but 300 hitter is a tag that means something today as it did in

00:34:10.470 --> 00:34:17.480
1930 as it did in 1890 so the statistics become a means by which we

00:34:17.480 --> 00:34:24.440
can connect to the permanence of this thing I can make that comparison with

00:34:24.440 --> 00:34:28.360
my father who could have made it with his father on that common language of

00:34:28.360 --> 00:34:28.760
statistics.

00:34:33.040 --> 00:34:41.160
On December 5th, 1856, Sunday New York Mercury referred to baseball for the first time,

00:34:41.500 --> 00:34:44.360
somewhat optimistically, as the national pastime.

00:34:46.360 --> 00:34:50.780
Ball playing communicated such an impulse to our limbs and joints

00:34:50.780 --> 00:34:56.800
that there is nothing now heard of in our leisure hours but ball, ball, ball.

00:34:58.930 --> 00:34:59.770
Can I prophesy?

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:07.020
any degree of accuracy concerning the continuance of this rage for play, but the effect is good

00:35:07.020 --> 00:35:15.580
since there's been a thoroughgoing reformation from inactivity and torpeteer. Henry Wadsworth

00:35:15.580 --> 00:35:28.850
Longfellow. There were some 50 clubs in the New York area alone by 1858.

00:35:30.380 --> 00:35:37.180
And special trains ran out to Long Island, where onlookers saw the New York All-Stars beat their Brooklyn counterparts

00:35:37.180 --> 00:35:43.970
and for the first time were made to pay for the privilege, 50 cents, to the man who owned the field.

00:35:45.100 --> 00:35:56.910
In an attempt to keep control of their game, the Knickerbockers and other established clubs banded together to form the National Association of Baseball Players.

00:35:56.950 --> 00:35:59.760
They set down still more rules.

00:36:01.020 --> 00:36:03.600
An umpire was given the power to call strikes.

00:36:05.130 --> 00:36:08.170
No one was allowed to catch the ball in his cap.

00:36:10.000 --> 00:36:13.580
Above all, baseball was to remain an amateur's game.

00:36:14.730 --> 00:36:16.830
No player was ever to be paid.

00:36:22.160 --> 00:36:26.430
By the spring of 1861, there were 62 member clubs

00:36:26.430 --> 00:36:29.090
in the National Association of Baseball Players.

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:33.300
Free blacks in northern cities had established their own teams.

00:36:34.460 --> 00:36:38.860
And Henry Chadwick was trying to start a baseball club in Richmond, Virginia,

00:36:39.220 --> 00:36:42.180
when the new season was suddenly interrupted.

00:36:51.460 --> 00:36:53.520
Virginia, April 3rd, 1862.

