00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:03.710
At first, the gamble seemed to pay off.

00:51:05.380 --> 00:51:08.240
Cincinnati scored two runs in the top of the 11th.

00:51:10.530 --> 00:51:12.650
But then, with two Atlantics on base,

00:51:12.810 --> 00:51:16.470
the Cincinnati first baseman, Charles Gould, made a bad throw.

00:51:19.750 --> 00:51:23.140
By the time it was all over, Brooklyn scored three runs,

00:51:23.220 --> 00:51:25.160
and Cincinnati had been beaten.

00:51:30.800 --> 00:51:34.360
June 14th, telegram to the Cincinnati commercial.

00:51:35.500 --> 00:51:38.070
Atlantic's eight, Cincinnati's seven.

00:51:39.060 --> 00:51:40.640
The finest game ever played.

00:51:41.320 --> 00:51:44.740
Our boys did nobly, but fortune was against us.

00:51:45.510 --> 00:51:47.360
Though beaten, not disgraced.

00:51:48.620 --> 00:51:49.120
Harry Wright.

00:51:51.590 --> 00:51:53.170
Cincinnati was devastated.

00:51:54.470 --> 00:51:55.810
With their winning streak over,

00:51:56.100 --> 00:51:58.440
fans stopped going to Red Stockings games.

00:52:01.120 --> 00:52:08.060
Investors withdrew their support, complaining that with attendance down, the players' salary demands were unreasonable.

00:52:11.980 --> 00:52:13.990
Finally, the team was disbanded.

00:52:20.560 --> 00:52:21.880
Harry Wright moved on.

00:52:24.700 --> 00:52:29.960
At the invitation of a band of New England promoters, he took the best of his red stockings.

00:52:30.160 --> 00:52:38.760
boston where they became the most successful team in the country by moving his stars from city to

00:52:38.760 --> 00:52:48.020
city the sporting times later said wright had set new prices on their muscle baseball is business now

00:52:48.020 --> 00:52:53.740
and i'm trying to arrange our games to make them successful and make them pay

00:52:54.880 --> 00:52:58.560
irrespective of my feelings and to the best of my ability

00:53:00.030 --> 00:53:03.670
If I should fail, then I will try and do better next time.

00:53:04.930 --> 00:53:05.390
Harry Wright.

00:53:07.590 --> 00:53:11.830
For the professional teams and leagues that now sprung up all across the country,

00:53:12.180 --> 00:53:17.290
winning and the profits it promised was fast becoming the most important thing.

00:53:19.840 --> 00:53:25.030
But outside the big cities, baseball remained a game, not a business.

00:53:27.320 --> 00:53:29.960
When we heard of a professional game in which...

00:53:30.000 --> 00:53:33.660
men cared nothing whatever for patriotism but only for money,

00:53:34.300 --> 00:53:39.240
games in which rival towns would hire the best players from a natural enemy,

00:53:39.790 --> 00:53:42.250
we could scarcely believe the tale was true.

00:53:43.200 --> 00:53:47.480
No kinsman boy would any more give aid and comfort to a rival town

00:53:47.480 --> 00:53:53.080
than would a loyal soldier open a gate in the wall and let an enemy march in.

00:53:54.440 --> 00:53:56.870
Clarence Darrow, Kinsman, Ohio.

00:54:01.630 --> 00:54:10.310
August 10th 1874 I was catcher for the Hartfords and Cherokee Fisher was

00:54:10.310 --> 00:54:15.930
pitching he is a lightning pitcher and very few could catch for him on that

00:54:15.930 --> 00:54:20.200
occasion he delivered as wicked a ball has ever left his hands and it went

00:54:20.200 --> 00:54:25.020
through my grasp like an express train striking me with full force inside I

00:54:26.490 --> 00:54:29.830
fell insensible to the ground but was quickly picked up

00:54:30.060 --> 00:54:32.140
placed in a carriage and driven to my hotel.

00:54:33.660 --> 00:54:35.420
The doctor who attended me gave me

00:54:35.420 --> 00:54:37.260
a hypodermic injection of morphine,

00:54:38.500 --> 00:54:40.340
but I had rather died behind the bat

00:54:40.720 --> 00:54:42.280
than have had that first dose.

00:54:44.340 --> 00:54:45.800
My injury was only temporary,

00:54:45.940 --> 00:54:48.340
but from taking prescriptions of morphine

00:54:48.340 --> 00:54:51.140
during my illness, the habit grew on me,

00:54:51.850 --> 00:54:53.930
and I am now powerless in its grasp.

00:54:55.990 --> 00:54:59.070
My morphine pleasure has cost me $8 a day at least.

00:55:01.470 --> 00:55:05.690
I was once catcher for the Mutuals, also for the Atlantics,

00:55:06.700 --> 00:55:08.940
but no one would think it to look at me now.

00:55:11.130 --> 00:55:13.080
Tom Barlow, Hartford Hartford.

00:55:30.060 --> 00:55:34.320
The aim of baseball is to employ professional players

00:55:34.320 --> 00:55:39.010
to perspire in public for the benefit of gamblers.

00:55:40.360 --> 00:55:41.460
The New York Times.

00:55:46.150 --> 00:55:51.120
By the mid-1870s, a man could make a good living playing baseball,

00:55:51.140 --> 00:55:56.820
but some found they could make an even better living throwing games for gamblers.

00:55:58.440 --> 00:55:59.980
Speculators trading on...

00:56:00.000 --> 00:56:05.650
side information had taken over much of the professional game, just as they had taken over

00:56:05.650 --> 00:56:12.530
many other institutions, cornering the gold market, ruining the stock market, defrauding

00:56:12.530 --> 00:56:20.640
the government, creating huge monopolies. As the country approached its centennial celebration,

00:56:21.520 --> 00:56:29.980
public faith and its national pastime began to fade. Henry Chadwick, who had struggled so hard

00:56:30.000 --> 00:56:36.850
hard to promote baseball, now found himself crusading against corruption in the game he loved.

00:56:38.880 --> 00:56:46.900
Baseball has fallen. Yes, the national game has become degraded. At certain match games,

00:56:47.020 --> 00:56:53.420
large amounts of money changed hands among the spectator. A noted New York club is said

00:56:53.420 --> 00:56:59.310
to have sold the results of a match. Barked chins and broken fingers may be easily mended.

00:57:00.220 --> 00:57:03.020
but a disfigured reputation may never be entirely repaired.

00:57:04.520 --> 00:57:07.980
Once more, abandon the back, boys, if you cannot keep the game pure.

00:57:12.000 --> 00:57:17.780
On February 2nd, 1876, at Manhattan's Grand Central Hotel,

00:57:18.690 --> 00:57:23.000
a group of club owners, eager to tighten their control of the game,

00:57:23.690 --> 00:57:28.170
restore its respectability, and most of all to ensure greater profits,

00:57:29.140 --> 00:57:29.980
started a new...

00:57:30.000 --> 00:57:35.870
They called it the National League of Professional Baseball Clubs.

00:57:39.250 --> 00:57:43.330
It is ridiculous to pay ballplayers $2,000 a year,

00:57:44.180 --> 00:57:47.660
especially when the $800 boys often do just as well.

00:57:48.730 --> 00:57:49.570
William Hulbert.

00:57:51.200 --> 00:57:53.940
William A. Hulbert, a ruthless coal magnate

00:57:53.940 --> 00:57:55.540
who owned the Chicago White Stockings,

00:57:55.970 --> 00:57:57.990
became the National League's president.

00:57:58.940 --> 00:57:59.980
He immediately took...

00:58:00.000 --> 00:58:07.160
steps to revive the reputation of the professional game players were forbidden to drink on the field

00:58:07.160 --> 00:58:15.380
or off no beer was to be served on the grounds gambling was barred ticket prices were set at 50

00:58:15.380 --> 00:58:23.070
cents and no games were to be played on sundays above all power was to be invested in the owners

00:58:23.070 --> 00:58:29.670
not the player. It's not that the owners came in for

00:58:30.000 --> 00:58:34.480
entirely bad reasons there were you know the questions about gambling in the game and other

00:58:34.480 --> 00:58:40.500
seedy aspects of it they wanted to clean it up but that's as much a historical cover story as it was

00:58:40.500 --> 00:58:49.930
a reality like all good american businessmen they saw an opportunity to further solidify their

00:58:49.930 --> 00:58:56.730
control the owners added a reserve clause to the contracts of the five best men on every team

00:58:57.040 --> 00:58:59.930
it required that each play

00:59:00.000 --> 00:59:05.360
only for his current employer and reserved his services for the following year.

00:59:05.800 --> 00:59:08.520
At first, few complained.

00:59:08.520 --> 00:59:12.370
To be reserved was to be sure of a job for the coming season.

00:59:13.700 --> 00:59:17.500
Those who did complain that the reserve clause smacked of slavery

00:59:17.500 --> 00:59:20.350
were fired, then blacklisted.

00:59:30.540 --> 00:59:36.520
For the first time in the history of the game, the players would serve the interests of the owners.

00:59:36.860 --> 00:59:45.900
For the next 100 years, the professional game would be dominated by those who owned the field and supplied the ball.

00:59:47.630 --> 00:59:49.840
Players would simply be employees.

01:00:05.340 --> 01:00:08.540
It is not known whether the players have been dissipating,

01:00:08.840 --> 01:00:11.940
keeping late hours, and having a jolly time generally.

01:00:13.240 --> 01:00:16.300
But tight or sober, they should realize the fact

01:00:16.300 --> 01:00:20.340
that they've run afoul of a most humiliating set of reverses.

01:00:21.270 --> 01:00:22.890
The Louisville Courier Journal.

01:00:25.480 --> 01:00:29.800
After a spectacular early season in 1877...

01:00:30.000 --> 01:00:35.700
The Louisville Grays of the new National League mysteriously lost seven games in a row.

01:00:36.850 --> 01:00:42.640
Players bobbled the ball, seemed to slow between bases, swung suspiciously wide.

01:00:44.120 --> 01:00:45.680
Grays lost the pennant.

01:00:46.780 --> 01:00:51.880
Afterwards, some were seen wearing fancy clothes and diamond stick pants.

01:00:54.350 --> 01:00:59.980
An investigation revealed that gamblers had bought off four players, including one of the national...

01:01:00.000 --> 01:01:01.680
of the National League's greatest pitchers,

01:01:02.060 --> 01:01:03.700
the popular Jim Devlin.

01:01:04.910 --> 01:01:08.080
When confronted with the evidence, Devlin confessed.

01:01:09.560 --> 01:01:11.520
I was introduced to a man named McLeod,

01:01:11.910 --> 01:01:14.510
who said when I wanted to make a little money to let him know,

01:01:16.240 --> 01:01:18.600
was to use the word sash in telegraphing,

01:01:18.660 --> 01:01:19.800
and he would know what was meant.

01:01:21.700 --> 01:01:24.020
We made a contract to throw a game in Indianapolis,

01:01:24.790 --> 01:01:28.520
received $100 from McLeod for it, and gave it to my wife.

01:01:30.480 --> 01:01:34.140
The magnitude of the conspiracy stunned the baseball world.

01:01:35.500 --> 01:01:38.060
The Louisville Grays suspended the accused players,

01:01:38.060 --> 01:01:43.080
who claimed they had only done it because their owners had failed to pay them their wages.

01:01:45.480 --> 01:01:48.740
Devlin was brought before National League president William Hulbert.

01:01:50.320 --> 01:01:54.060
Devlin was in tears. Hulbert was in tears.

01:01:56.000 --> 01:01:59.980
I saw Hulbert take a $50 bill and press it into the...

01:02:00.000 --> 01:02:07.780
arm of the prostrate player. And then I heard him say, that's what I think of you personally.

01:02:08.840 --> 01:02:17.280
But damn you, Devlin, you are dishonest. You have sold a game, and I can't trust you. Now go

01:02:17.280 --> 01:02:23.500
and let me never see your face again, for your act will not be condoned so long as I live.

01:02:24.880 --> 01:02:29.980
Albert Goodwill Spalding. Despite their pleas for forgiveness,

01:02:30.000 --> 01:02:39.410
hulbert banned all four players from baseball forever for five years jim devlin haunted the

01:02:39.410 --> 01:02:45.540
corridors outside meetings of the national league club owners hoping somehow to be reinstated

01:02:47.640 --> 01:02:55.610
desperate he wrote to harry wright still the most respected man in the game mr harry wright

01:02:56.500 --> 01:02:59.810
dear sir as i am deprived from

01:03:00.000 --> 01:03:04.630
playing this year. I thought I would write you to see if you could do anything for me in the way of

01:03:04.630 --> 01:03:11.590
looking after your ground or anything in the way of work. I don't know what I'm to do. I can assure

01:03:11.590 --> 01:03:18.670
you, Harry, that I was not treated right. I am honest, Harry. You need not be afraid. The Louisville

01:03:18.670 --> 01:03:25.560
people made me what I am today, a beggar. I have not got a stitch of clothing or has my wife and

01:03:25.560 --> 01:03:29.960
child. I am dumb, Harry. I don't know.

01:03:30.000 --> 01:03:36.770
know how to go about it so i trust you will answer this and do all you can for me so i

01:03:36.770 --> 01:03:42.210
still close by sending you and george and all the boys my very best wishes hoping to hear from

01:03:42.210 --> 01:03:58.760
you soon i'm yours truly james a devlin very right did nothing devlin got a job as a policeman in 1880

01:04:00.000 --> 01:04:06.770
of consumption just three years later his early death said a louisville newspaper was

01:04:06.860 --> 01:04:16.560
an instructive example of the fruits of crookedness the national league had survived its first scandal

01:04:27.370 --> 01:04:29.790
baseball has these absolutely unique sounds

01:04:30.220 --> 01:04:37.870
um the sounds of spring of summer i i can remember a shortstop uh i don't remember his name now but

01:04:37.870 --> 01:04:42.370
he used i used to pitch and he used to call out he'd be he'd be he'd be he'd be and it would sort

01:04:42.370 --> 01:04:47.700
of drift out across the fields um you know the sound of the ball against the bat is absolutely

