extraordinary brings back i don't know any american male that doesn't hear that in the

01:04:52.300 --> 01:04:58.670
springtime and get called back to some some moment in the past or some or summer days in the past.

01:05:09.600 --> 01:05:16.240
Despite growing professionalism, the pure game survived in thousands of small towns all across the country.

01:05:18.380 --> 01:05:22.650
Sam Crawford, who would one day become one of the best outfielders in the game,

01:05:23.170 --> 01:05:26.620
first played on a traveling town team from Wahoo, Nebraska.

01:05:28.660 --> 01:05:29.960
I remember when I made my...

01:05:30.000 --> 01:05:36.680
first baseball trip, a bunch of us from around Wahoo made a trip overland in a wagon drawn by

01:05:36.680 --> 01:05:43.550
a team of horses. I think there were 11 or 12 of us, and we just started out and went from town to

01:05:43.550 --> 01:05:51.190
town. One of the boys was a carnet player, and when we'd come to a town, he'd whip out that

01:05:51.190 --> 01:05:57.910
carnet and sound off. People would all come out to see what was going on, and we'd announce that

01:05:57.910 --> 01:05:59.980
we were the Wahoo team and were ready to go.

01:06:00.000 --> 01:06:06.830
for the ball game every little town out there on the prairie had its own ball team and ball grounds

01:06:07.050 --> 01:06:14.690
and we challenged them all it wasn't easy to win those games as you can imagine each of those towns

01:06:14.690 --> 01:06:22.600
had its own umpire so you really had to go some to win we were gone three or four weeks we take up

01:06:22.600 --> 01:06:28.380
a collection at the games pass the hat you know and that paid our expenses or some of them anyway

01:06:30.480 --> 01:06:34.030
One of the boys was the cook, but all he could cook was round steak.

01:06:34.740 --> 01:06:38.000
We'd get 12 pounds for a dollar and have a feast.

01:06:39.040 --> 01:06:43.160
We'd drive along the country roads, and if we came to a stream, we'd go swimming.

01:06:44.140 --> 01:06:46.980
If we came to an apple orchard, we'd fill up on apples.

01:06:47.760 --> 01:06:52.690
We'd sleep anywhere, sometimes in a tent, lots of times on the ground, out in the open.

01:06:53.510 --> 01:06:56.270
If we were near some fairgrounds, we'd slip in there.

01:06:56.690 --> 01:06:59.360
If we were near a barn, well...

01:07:00.640 --> 01:07:02.660
Sam Crawford, Wahoo, Nebraska.

01:07:04.000 --> 01:07:15.390
In 1878, more than 8 million bats were sold in the United States,

01:07:15.940 --> 01:07:21.480
and players often rode to the ball field in painted carriages, singing team songs.

01:07:23.260 --> 01:07:27.520
Men who were crazy about baseball were called bugs and cranks.

01:07:28.830 --> 01:07:29.970
Women who shared their...

01:07:30.000 --> 01:07:36.930
Their excitement were cranklets, later they would be called fans, short either for baseball

01:07:36.930 --> 01:07:39.220
fanciers or fanatics.

01:07:41.130 --> 01:07:42.490
Everybody had a team.

01:07:43.990 --> 01:07:47.170
At Orting, Washington, the Fats took on the lean.

01:07:50.430 --> 01:07:56.000
In Kansas, crowds watched the Mother Hubbards, men playing in women's clothing.

01:07:58.490 --> 01:07:59.970
Under guard at Fort Sill.

01:08:00.000 --> 01:08:10.660
Oklahoma, Geronimo's Chiricahua Apaches played ball against the U.S. Army and won. Whalers,

01:08:10.870 --> 01:08:18.360
frozen in at Herschel Island above the Arctic Circle, played two, naming their teams the Hoodlums,

01:08:18.830 --> 01:08:27.640
Walruses, and Blubbers. At some games, a keg of beer stood just to the side of third base

01:08:27.640 --> 01:08:29.150
for encouragement.

01:08:30.510 --> 01:08:38.040
any man who made it to third was entitled to a dipper full when the university of illinois

01:08:38.040 --> 01:08:45.460
played northwestern illinois rooters fired blanks into the air much more convenient than yelling

01:08:45.460 --> 01:08:55.180
the campus paper said and has a better effect on the visiting team when the princeton tigers beat

01:08:55.180 --> 01:08:58.580
their fans roared right onto the field.

01:09:12.280 --> 01:09:17.440
It says, I think, that at root, we're children, or we'd like to be.

01:09:18.810 --> 01:09:25.180
And the best of us keep as much of that childhood with us as we grow into adulthood as we can muster.

01:09:25.580 --> 01:09:29.980
And the most creative, the most happy, the most fortunate of us are the...

01:09:30.000 --> 01:09:32.040
those who don't lose the sense of play.

01:09:32.260 --> 01:09:34.160
And even after we're past the point

01:09:34.160 --> 01:09:36.400
of being able to actually play the game with any skill,

01:09:37.710 --> 01:09:39.170
if we love it, then

01:09:39.170 --> 01:09:41.400
it's like Peter Pan. We remain

01:09:41.400 --> 01:09:43.100
boys forever. We don't die.

01:09:54.220 --> 01:09:55.800
Baseball is the very symbol,

01:09:56.140 --> 01:09:57.920
the outward and visible expression

01:09:57.920 --> 01:09:59.960
of the drive and push and...

01:10:00.000 --> 01:10:12.310
rush and struggle of the raging, tearing, booming 19th century. Mark Twain. In 1882,

01:10:12.610 --> 01:10:18.230
owners of Midwestern clubs left out of the National League established a league of their own,

01:10:18.580 --> 01:10:26.120
the American Baseball Association. Its games cost just a quarter, its teams played on Sundays,

01:10:26.500 --> 01:10:28.740
and its ballparks sold liquor.

01:10:30.000 --> 01:10:33.720
The new Beer and Whiskey League drew bigger, rowdier crowds.

01:10:34.240 --> 01:10:36.580
The stands filled with working men and immigrants,

01:10:37.010 --> 01:10:39.290
not the middle-class, native-born fans

01:10:39.290 --> 01:10:41.110
who followed the National League.

01:10:43.370 --> 01:10:44.990
It was baseball's heyday.

01:10:45.710 --> 01:10:48.240
Competition flourished, play improved,

01:10:49.150 --> 01:10:50.670
attendance skyrocketed,

01:10:52.980 --> 01:10:55.420
and huge wood and iron grandstands

01:10:55.420 --> 01:10:57.080
were built in all the big cities.

01:11:00.920 --> 01:11:07.280
The two leagues staged an end-of-the-season championship for fans who could not get enough baseball.

01:11:12.100 --> 01:11:24.580
In 1882, William Hulbert died, and control of the National League and his old team, the Chicago White Stockings, passed to his second-in-command, A.G. Spaulding.

01:11:26.820 --> 01:11:29.960
The magnet must be a strong man amongst...

01:11:30.000 --> 01:11:41.420
men. Everything is possible to him who dares. Albert Goodwill Spalding. The railroads had

01:11:41.420 --> 01:11:50.520
Commodore Vanderbilt. Big Steel had Andrew Carnegie. Big Oil, John D. Rockefeller. Baseball

01:11:50.520 --> 01:11:58.900
had Albert Goodwill Spalding. He had been the finest pitcher of the 1870s.

01:12:00.190 --> 01:12:08.440
He learned his baseball from Harry Wright, who had paid him $1,500 a year to pitch for the Boston Red Stockings.

01:12:11.880 --> 01:12:21.860
In 1876, he left Boston for Chicago, lured by William Hulbert's offer of a $500 raise and 25% of the gate.

01:12:23.450 --> 01:12:26.090
But Spalding had still bigger things in mind.

01:12:26.740 --> 01:12:29.870
At age 27, he stopped pitching entirely.

01:12:30.000 --> 01:12:34.580
to become a full-time promoter of baseball and himself.

01:12:37.020 --> 01:12:41.420
With $800 borrowed from his mother, he opened a sporting goods business.

01:12:42.240 --> 01:12:46.800
Soon, he was manufacturing all the baseballs used in the National League.

01:12:48.510 --> 01:12:51.170
He then began making bats and uniforms,

01:12:52.300 --> 01:12:58.780
managing to persuade club owners that each position should have its own distinctive garb.

01:13:00.000 --> 01:13:06.380
result was chaos. The team looked like a Dutch bed of tulips, a Chicago sports writer said,

01:13:07.150 --> 01:13:20.950
and the experiment was quickly abandoned. Like other captains of industry, Spalding crushed

01:13:20.950 --> 01:13:26.820
or bought out his competitor, becoming the largest sporting goods manufacturer in the country.

01:13:30.030 --> 01:13:41.640
sporting goods, its proprietor said, is converting all America to the gospel of exercise. Balding

01:13:41.640 --> 01:13:48.300
ran his team and his empire from the private box he built for himself in Chicago's Congress

01:13:48.300 --> 01:13:54.730
Street grounds, fitted out with a gong to summon servants, and a new invention, a telephone,

01:13:55.430 --> 01:13:59.130
to keep track of all his enterprises while he watched the game.

01:14:04.960 --> 01:14:08.320
Newspapers called him the baseball messiah.

01:14:16.590 --> 01:14:21.810
Only one continent now remains to be subjugated by the American baseball bat.

01:14:23.070 --> 01:14:27.410
Australia surrendered after a three-weeks campaign of great brilliancy.

01:14:29.080 --> 01:14:29.880
Asia was met.

01:14:30.000 --> 01:14:31.540
and overcome at Colombo.

01:14:32.700 --> 01:14:34.780
Africa sent her forces up the Nile,

01:14:35.500 --> 01:14:38.480
only to be overcome and brought to terms

01:14:38.480 --> 01:14:40.220
in the shadow of the Great Pyramid.

01:14:42.160 --> 01:14:44.540
At the end of the 1888 season,

01:14:44.920 --> 01:14:46.840
Spaulding led his white stockings

01:14:46.840 --> 01:14:50.300
and a pickup team of all-stars on a round-the-world tour

01:14:50.300 --> 01:14:53.420
to spread the gospel of the Great American Game.

01:14:56.200 --> 01:14:57.500
We are up at breakfast early,

01:14:57.520 --> 01:14:59.820
as we are to start at 10 o'clock for the pyramids.

01:15:00.730 --> 01:15:03.030
Camels and donkeys have been secured for the party,

01:15:03.670 --> 01:15:04.930
the ballplayers in uniform,

01:15:04.950 --> 01:15:08.430
as for the first time the Sphinx is to witness a game of baseball.

01:15:11.300 --> 01:15:13.620
After lunch, we have photos taken at the Sphinx

01:15:13.620 --> 01:15:17.020
and then proceed to play our historical game of ball

01:15:17.020 --> 01:15:19.640
with about 200 Arabs for an audience.

01:15:21.220 --> 01:15:23.740
They took more interest in the game than the average Englishman

01:15:23.740 --> 01:15:28.230
and did not once refer to it as the old game of rounders, you know.

01:15:30.480 --> 01:15:35.570
In March, the tour arrived in England.

01:15:37.290 --> 01:15:41.350
The verdict of the spectators is almost universally against it,

01:15:41.640 --> 01:15:43.940
as a competitor with our national game.

01:15:45.100 --> 01:15:49.420
And in our own individual judgment, it has so many inherent defects

01:15:49.420 --> 01:15:53.220
that it has not the slightest pretensions to be considered superior to,

01:15:53.540 --> 01:15:59.200
even if it is equal with, our own juvenile amusement rounders.

01:16:00.090 --> 01:16:01.990
on the basis of which it has been modeled.

01:16:05.870 --> 01:16:07.490
When the tour got back to New York,

01:16:07.650 --> 01:16:11.390
there was a banquet, served in nine innings, at Delmonico's.

01:16:11.830 --> 01:16:14.820
Theodore Roosevelt attended, Mark Twain spoke,

01:16:16.220 --> 01:16:18.160
and when the president of the National League

01:16:18.160 --> 01:16:20.320
declared that patriotism and research

01:16:20.320 --> 01:16:23.700
had shown that baseball was a purely American invention,

01:16:24.340 --> 01:16:26.280
guests and players alike chanted,

01:16:26.540 --> 01:16:28.940
No rounders, no rounders.

01:16:32.140 --> 01:16:34.800
In the end, Spaulding lost money on the tour,

01:16:35.190 --> 01:16:39.190
and baseball failed to catch on anywhere his teams had played.

01:16:56.860 --> 01:16:59.970
On many summer days, I played baseball starting...

01:17:00.000 --> 01:17:06.220
at eight in the morning, running home at noon for a quick meal, and again with fielding and batting

01:17:06.220 --> 01:17:15.050
till it was too dark to see the ball. These were times when my head seemed empty of everything but

01:17:15.050 --> 01:17:23.160
baseball names and figures. I could name the players who led in batting and fielding, and the

01:17:23.160 --> 01:17:29.970
pitchers who had won the most games, and I had my opinions about who was better than any.

01:17:30.000 --> 01:17:43.670
else in the national game, Carl Sandburg. By the 1880s, newspapers and magazines

01:17:43.670 --> 01:17:49.140
and now baseball cards printed up to help boost the sale of cigarettes, brought

01:17:49.140 --> 01:17:53.640
into the homes of young boys a generation of baseball heroes they would

01:17:53.640 --> 01:17:59.760
never get to see in person. Pete Browning, the old gladiator of the Louisville

01:17:59.760 --> 01:17:59.960
Eclipses.

01:18:00.000 --> 01:18:03.650
had a lifetime batting average of .343

01:18:03.650 --> 01:18:06.280
and was the idol of Kentucky fans.

01:18:07.520 --> 01:18:11.220
One day in 1884, he broke his favorite bat.

01:18:12.930 --> 01:18:16.250
After the game, an apprentice woodworker named Bud Hillerick

01:18:16.500 --> 01:18:18.520
offered to make Browning a new bat.

01:18:19.700 --> 01:18:21.500
The next day, Browning went .343.

01:18:23.080 --> 01:18:25.380
Thereafter, he would use no one else's bat.

01:18:27.370 --> 01:18:29.690
It was the first Louisville slugger.

01:18:30.280 --> 01:18:33.620
and Browning would eventually own more than 200 of them,

01:18:34.090 --> 01:18:37.250
to each of which he gave a name taken from the Bible.

01:18:40.250 --> 01:18:44.690
Roger Connor of the New York Giants was the era's greatest home run hitter.

01:18:46.660 --> 01:18:49.820
He smashed 138 during his career,

01:18:50.630 --> 01:18:54.320
a record which would stand until Babe Ruth came along.

01:19:02.360 --> 01:19:06.400
Denton True Young was an ungainly, out-of-place farm boy

01:19:06.400 --> 01:19:09.820
when he came to the Cleveland Spiders in 1890.

01:19:11.350 --> 01:19:15.440
Even his teammates took to calling him Cy, short for Cyrus,

01:19:15.850 --> 01:19:17.690
because he seemed so countrified.

01:19:20.060 --> 01:19:22.840
But Cy Young held the Chicago White Stockings

01:19:22.840 --> 01:19:25.760
to just three hits to win his first game

01:19:25.760 --> 01:19:29.850
and then went on to win 510 more.

01:19:30.000 --> 01:19:34.260
before he was through, a record never even approached

01:19:34.260 --> 01:19:35.920
by any other pitcher.

01:19:38.380 --> 01:19:41.010
Cy came to stand for Cyclone.

01:19:46.510 --> 01:19:48.390
Mike Kelly was the trickiest player

01:19:48.390 --> 01:19:49.690
who ever handled a baseball.

01:19:50.190 --> 01:19:52.110
There was nothing he would not attempt.

01:19:53.110 --> 01:19:55.610
Baseball rules were never made for Kell.

01:19:57.450 --> 01:19:59.970
The most popular and most notorious star

01:20:00.000 --> 01:20:03.860
of the 19th century was Michael Joseph King Kelly,

01:20:04.660 --> 01:20:07.500
the sure-handed catcher for the Chicago White Stockings.

01:20:08.540 --> 01:20:10.460
He was so skilled at stealing bases,

01:20:10.660 --> 01:20:13.140
he once stole six in a single game,

01:20:13.470 --> 01:20:15.390
that he inspired a popular song,

01:20:15.550 --> 01:20:16.970
Slide, Kelly, Slide.

01:20:17.330 --> 01:20:21.090
He sometimes cut across the diamond,

01:20:21.350 --> 01:20:24.450
skipping second altogether when the umpire was not looking.

01:20:26.460 --> 01:20:28.560
Kelly drank as hard as he competed.

01:20:30.000 --> 01:20:33.540
Once, A.G. Spaulding put Pinkerton detectives on his trail

01:20:33.540 --> 01:20:38.200
and accused him of having been in a saloon at 3 a.m. drinking lemonade.

01:20:39.800 --> 01:20:40.820
Kelly was indignant.

01:20:41.460 --> 01:20:43.080
It was straight whiskey, he said.

01:20:43.080 --> 01:20:46.180
I never drank a lemonade at that hour in my life.

01:20:48.170 --> 01:20:51.650
Kelly and other carousing Chicago players were widely blamed

01:20:51.650 --> 01:20:55.930
for losing the championship to St. Louis in 1886.

01:20:59.100 --> 01:20:59.900
That winter...

01:21:00.000 --> 01:21:03.340
an exasperated Spalding sold Kelly to Boston

01:21:03.340 --> 01:21:06.670
for the unheard-of sum of $10,000.

01:21:07.540 --> 01:21:09.540
Chicago fans were devastated,

01:21:10.040 --> 01:21:12.080
but Boston fans were so delighted

01:21:12.080 --> 01:21:14.580
they presented their new star with a house

01:21:14.580 --> 01:21:17.600
and a carriage drawn by two white horses

01:21:17.600 --> 01:21:19.720
in which to ride to the ballpark.

01:21:21.510 --> 01:21:24.560
I love King Kelly. I just love King Kelly.

01:21:25.970 --> 01:21:27.490
One of the great stories, my favorite,

01:21:28.020 --> 01:21:29.680
is the day when he was sitting on the bench.

01:21:30.030 --> 01:21:33.010
And the rule at that time was that if you wanted to substitute for a player,

01:21:33.090 --> 01:21:34.210
all you had to do was announce yourself.

01:21:34.930 --> 01:21:36.910
So a foul ball comes in the direction of the bench.

01:21:37.230 --> 01:21:39.830
Kelly stands up, yells out, Kelly now catching for Boston.

01:21:40.230 --> 01:21:42.730
Catches the ball, and it's reported as an out.

01:21:44.740 --> 01:21:47.600
This is the trickster.

01:21:47.720 --> 01:21:49.200
This is the villain.

01:21:49.340 --> 01:21:50.080
This is the fool.

01:21:50.100 --> 01:21:52.020
He is also a great, great player.

01:21:52.120 --> 01:21:56.950
He is all the wonderful archetypes of baseball wrapped into one.

01:21:57.110 --> 01:21:59.840
And he also managed to drink himself to death.

01:22:00.000 --> 01:22:09.510
he hit the age of 40 so that's also an archetype alas in baseball i was a natural born kicker

01:22:09.950 --> 01:22:15.540
been upon making trouble for others i had an instinctive dislike both the study and work

01:22:15.540 --> 01:22:23.290
and i shirked them whenever the opportunity offered cap anson his real name was adrian

01:22:23.290 --> 01:22:29.530
constantine anson but those who cheered him for 27 years knew him first as baby

01:22:30.000 --> 01:22:32.430
then his cap, and finally his pop.

01:22:33.690 --> 01:22:36.310
He was the greatest player of his century.

01:22:38.180 --> 01:22:41.940
Anson played every position but pitcher for the Philadelphia Athletics,

01:22:42.480 --> 01:22:45.400
then joined Spalding's Chicago White Stockings

01:22:45.400 --> 01:22:48.010
and became first baseman and captain.

01:22:49.670 --> 01:22:53.170
He batted over 300 for 20 consecutive seasons,

01:22:53.770 --> 01:22:56.610
drove in 1,700 runs,

01:22:56.810 --> 01:22:59.980
and was the first man ever to accuse him of.

01:23:00.000 --> 01:23:10.080
3,000 hits. Chicago fans loved Anson, but his players did not. He was too stern a

01:23:10.080 --> 01:23:15.970
taskmaster for that, imposing bed checks, levying $100 fines for beer drinking,

01:23:16.450 --> 01:23:22.040
insisting they report for a new early season workout he called spring training.

01:23:23.840 --> 01:23:27.740
But they admired him for the skills he taught them and his overwhelming

01:23:27.740 --> 01:23:29.200
determination to win.

01:23:30.510 --> 01:23:36.990
Cap Anson was the symbol, one writer said, of all that was strong and good in baseball.

01:23:37.290 --> 01:23:55.600
If anywhere in this world the social barriers are broken down, it is on the ball field.

01:23:56.580 --> 01:23:59.840
There, many men of low birth and poor breeding.

01:24:00.410 --> 01:24:09.240
the idols of the rich and cultured. The best man is he who plays best. In view of

01:24:09.240 --> 01:24:16.100
these facts, the objection to colored men is ridiculous. If social distinctions are

01:24:16.100 --> 01:24:22.340
to be made, half the players in the country will be shut out. Better make

01:24:22.340 --> 01:24:27.500
character and personal habits the test. Newark Call.

01:24:30.600 --> 01:24:37.580
African Americans, freed from slavery by the Civil War and filled with hope for a better future and freedom,

01:24:37.920 --> 01:24:45.000
soon found themselves prisoners again of white prejudice in the North and of Jim Crow laws in the South

01:24:45.470 --> 01:24:54.040
that segregated every aspect of their lives, even games of baseball at an orphanage.

01:24:57.320 --> 01:24:59.860
But freed blacks formed their own baseball.

01:25:00.000 --> 01:25:06.540
clubs in cities north and south. One of Frederick Douglass' sons played for a team in Washington,

01:25:06.760 --> 01:25:16.520
D.C. And more than 50 blacks played professional baseball alongside whites during the 1870s

01:25:16.520 --> 01:25:26.270
and 80s. But it was never easy. Ball players do not burn with the desire to have colored

01:25:26.270 --> 01:25:29.960
men on the team. It is, in fact, the deep sea.

01:25:30.000 --> 01:25:35.420
objection to Afro-Americans that gave rise to the feet-first slide. The

01:25:35.420 --> 01:25:40.400
Buffaloes had a Negro for second base. He was a few shades blacker than a raven,

01:25:40.640 --> 01:25:45.410
but was one of the best players in the Eastern League. The players of the

01:25:45.410 --> 01:25:51.620
opposing team made it a point to spite this brunette Buffalo. They would tarry

01:25:51.620 --> 01:25:55.970
at second when they might easily make third, just to toy with the sensitive

01:25:55.970 --> 01:25:59.900
shins of the second baseman. The poor man

01:26:00.000 --> 01:26:06.680
played only two games out of five. The rest of the time he was on crutches, sporting life.

01:26:09.200 --> 01:26:16.780
In 1884, the son of a black Ohio clergyman, Moses Fleetwood Walker, became the first African

01:26:16.780 --> 01:26:23.560
American to make it all the way to the majors. Walker joined the Toledo Blue Stockings of the

01:26:23.560 --> 01:26:29.060
American Association as a catcher and immediately ran into a wall of bigotry.

01:26:31.120 --> 01:26:37.660
The Irish pitcher, Tony Mullane, ignored Walker's signals because he said he wouldn't take orders from a black man.

01:26:39.900 --> 01:26:43.920
Cap Anson himself tried to have Walker ejected from an exhibition game,

01:26:44.380 --> 01:26:47.700
threatening not to play if they didn't get that nigger off the field.

01:26:48.950 --> 01:26:54.340
Anson only backed down when he realized he'd forfeit his pay if he really did walk out.

01:26:56.440 --> 01:26:59.960
At one game, the Toledo manager received a letter said to...

01:27:00.000 --> 01:27:06.250
from 75 determined men who threatened to mob Fleet Walker if he dared make an appearance.

01:27:07.770 --> 01:27:20.320
Walker kept playing. His brother, Welday, joined the team for a time. 1887 signaled the beginning

01:27:20.320 --> 01:27:29.120
of the end for black Americans in organized white baseball. When it seemed likely that the New York

01:27:29.120 --> 01:27:29.980
Giants would hire...

01:27:30.000 --> 01:27:36.880
the black pitcher George Stovey, Cap Anson made it clear that neither he nor any of his white

01:27:36.880 --> 01:27:46.200
stockings would ever play a team on which blacks were welcome. Just why Adrian C. Anson was so

01:27:46.200 --> 01:27:53.520
strongly opposed to colored players on white teams cannot be explained. His repugnant feeling

01:27:53.520 --> 01:27:59.630
toward colored ball players and his opposition with his great power and popularity.

01:28:00.000 --> 01:28:05.020
baseball circles hastened the exclusion of the black men from the white leagues,

01:28:06.400 --> 01:28:13.320
Sal White. Rather than face a revolt by Anson and other white players, the

01:28:13.320 --> 01:28:19.860
National League owners made a gentleman's agreement to sign No More Blacks. The

01:28:19.880 --> 01:28:24.540
minor leagues followed suit, formally declaring that black players would no

01:28:24.540 --> 01:28:29.980
longer be welcome. Almost overnight, Moses Fleetwood Walker...

01:28:30.000 --> 01:28:32.220
and all the other black players

01:28:32.500 --> 01:28:35.400
disappeared from organized white baseball.

01:28:36.130 --> 01:28:42.380
A few years later, the United States Supreme Court itself

01:28:42.380 --> 01:28:46.520
would rule that racial segregation was legal everywhere.

01:28:46.520 --> 01:28:51.410
It would be 60 years before another black man

01:28:51.410 --> 01:28:52.910
played in the major leagues.

01:28:55.380 --> 01:28:57.280
If I had not been quite so black,

01:28:57.980 --> 01:28:59.980
I might have caught on as a Spaniard or something.

01:29:00.750 --> 01:29:06.000
My skin is against me, Bud Fowler.

01:29:12.300 --> 01:29:14.740
You see a guy hit the ball out of the ballpark,

01:29:15.750 --> 01:29:19.660
a grand slam home run to win a baseball game, see?

01:29:20.340 --> 01:29:24.640
And that same guy can come up tomorrow in that situation

01:29:24.640 --> 01:29:27.690
and miss the ball and lose the ball game.

01:29:29.420 --> 01:29:29.920
It's...it...it...it...it...

01:29:30.000 --> 01:29:34.000
It can bring you up here, and don't get too damn cocky,

01:29:34.080 --> 01:29:37.750
because tomorrow it can bring you down there, see?

01:29:38.030 --> 01:29:43.400
But one thing about it, though, you know it always will be a tomorrow.

01:29:43.720 --> 01:29:46.350
You got me today, but I'm coming back.

01:29:56.480 --> 01:29:59.710
Baseball is good, an honorable profession.

01:30:00.380 --> 01:30:09.560
a great challenge. It has blessed me, I blessed it, and it has blessed our country, Branch Rickey.

01:30:12.750 --> 01:30:20.700
He was born on an Ohio farm in 1881 and named Wesley Branch Rickey for John Wesley,

01:30:20.920 --> 01:30:28.270
the founder of his family's Methodist faith. He was a pious, hardworking boy who memorized scripture,

01:30:28.270 --> 01:30:29.650
taught himself Greek.

01:30:30.000 --> 01:30:38.100
latin and algebra and promised his mother never to drink or swear or violate the sabbath but he

01:30:38.100 --> 01:30:43.820
was mad about baseball learned to play in the backyard with a ball stitched by his mother

01:30:44.260 --> 01:30:50.600
followed every move of the cincinnati reds and became a good enough catcher with the duck run

01:30:50.600 --> 01:30:59.480
ohio team to consider a professional career still the game was not a proper livelihood for a god

01:30:59.480 --> 01:30:59.960
fearing

01:31:00.000 --> 01:31:11.540
young man. And so, he abandoned baseball to teach school for $40 a month in Turkey Creek, Ohio. But

01:31:11.540 --> 01:31:17.860
Branch Rickey would one day become what one sports writer called the most original mind and

01:31:17.910 --> 01:31:28.100
the best organizer the game has ever produced. And in 1947, he would help make baseball in truth

01:31:28.100 --> 01:31:29.900
what it had always claimed.

01:31:30.000 --> 01:31:32.590
to be the national pastime.

01:31:38.300 --> 01:31:41.920
1889, if I cannot get my release,

01:31:42.200 --> 01:31:44.460
I must protect myself in another way.

01:31:45.820 --> 01:31:48.160
I'm over 40 and my fielding ain't so good,

01:31:48.820 --> 01:31:50.180
though I can still hit some.

01:31:51.510 --> 01:31:55.660
But I will say this, no man is going to sell my carcass

01:31:55.850 --> 01:31:57.190
unless I get half.

01:31:58.240 --> 01:31:59.980
Deacon White, Pittsburgh Allegheny.

01:32:00.000 --> 01:32:14.270
There was a time when the National League stood for integrity and fair dealing.

01:32:15.150 --> 01:32:16.970
Today it stands for dollars and cents.

01:32:18.800 --> 01:32:23.080
Once it looked to the elevation of the game and an honest exhibition of the sport.

01:32:24.020 --> 01:32:25.900
Today its eyes are on the turnstile.

01:32:27.500 --> 01:32:29.830
Players have been bought, sold.

01:32:30.280 --> 01:32:34.610
and exchanged as though they were sheep instead of American citizens.

01:32:35.710 --> 01:32:37.110
John Montgomery Ward.

01:32:37.960 --> 01:32:46.620
New York Giants second baseman John Montgomery Ward was a rarity among players,

01:32:46.620 --> 01:32:49.200
a graduate of the Columbia Law School.

01:32:50.370 --> 01:32:54.570
And in an age when workers everywhere were struggling to win their rights,

01:32:55.430 --> 01:32:58.790
he was willing to take on the club owners on their own terms.

01:33:00.090 --> 01:33:06.350
ward publicly denounced the reserve clause which now kept every player from deciding for himself

01:33:06.350 --> 01:33:12.380
where he wished to play and forced him to accept whatever wage his owner was willing to pay

01:33:12.380 --> 01:33:21.040
there is now no escape for the player if he attempts to elude the operation of the rule

01:33:21.040 --> 01:33:28.470
he becomes at once a professional outlaw and the hand of every club is against him like a fugitive

01:33:28.470 --> 01:33:29.940
slave law, the reserve.

01:33:30.000 --> 01:33:35.820
Clause denies him a harbor or a livelihood and carries him back bound and shackled to the club

01:33:35.820 --> 01:33:42.860
from which he attempted to escape. We have then the curious result of a contract which on its face

01:33:42.860 --> 01:33:53.120
is for seven months being binding for life. John Montgomery Ward. At a meeting in New York City

01:33:53.200 --> 01:33:59.730
Ward helped found the brotherhood of professional baseball players. It was the player's first

01:34:00.000 --> 01:34:05.140
to organize and they were determined to abolish the hated reserve clause.

01:34:07.870 --> 01:34:12.250
But Albert Goodwill Spalding and the other owners would not give an inch.

01:34:13.190 --> 01:34:21.270
In 1889, they tried further to consolidate their power by setting an absolute salary cap of $2,500.

01:34:22.060 --> 01:34:27.920
Then added insult to injury by charging the players rent for their uniforms.

01:34:27.920 --> 01:34:29.980
It was the last

01:34:30.000 --> 01:34:34.900
With the help of several would-be owners,

01:34:35.460 --> 01:34:38.120
Ward and the Brotherhood started a rival league,

01:34:38.560 --> 01:34:39.360
the Players League.

01:35:01.600 --> 01:35:03.680
At first, the new league did well.

01:35:05.080 --> 01:35:10.670
Fifty-six top players defected to its teams and brought their fans with them.

01:35:15.280 --> 01:35:17.710
I am for war without quarter.

01:35:19.230 --> 01:35:21.990
I want to fight until one of us drops dead.

01:35:23.720 --> 01:35:28.050
From this point on, it will simply be a case of dog-eat-dog.

01:35:29.240 --> 01:35:29.980
And the dog...

01:35:30.000 --> 01:35:33.150
with the bulldog tendencies will live the longest.

01:35:34.230 --> 01:35:36.250
Albert Goodwill Spalding.

01:35:38.280 --> 01:35:41.800
Spalding fought back by mounting costly lawsuits,

01:35:42.220 --> 01:35:45.380
lowering ticket prices, scheduling games

01:35:45.380 --> 01:35:47.340
to coincide with the players league,

01:35:48.280 --> 01:35:51.680
and by threatening to blacklist any man who dared

01:35:51.680 --> 01:35:55.330
play for the enemy while simultaneously trying

01:35:55.360 --> 01:35:59.210
to lure the new league's biggest stars back with bribes.

01:36:00.920 --> 01:36:03.160
King Kelly turned down $10,000.

01:36:04.100 --> 01:36:08.180
I need the money, he told Spalding, but I can't go back on the boys.

01:36:09.030 --> 01:36:11.650
The Players League defiantly held out.

01:36:14.420 --> 01:36:18.480
But three big leagues, the National League, the Players League,

01:36:18.580 --> 01:36:22.220
and the old Beer and Whiskey League, simply proved too many.

01:36:23.280 --> 01:36:26.140
Attendance dipped dangerously low for everyone.

01:36:29.010 --> 01:36:29.970
Finally, despite...

01:36:30.000 --> 01:36:37.070
his own precarious financial position. Spalding made one last gamble. Feigning a self-confidence

01:36:37.070 --> 01:36:44.570
he did not feel, he demanded unconditional surrender. The Players League could not hold

01:36:44.570 --> 01:36:54.300
out any longer. By the end of the 1890 season, the league had lost more than $340,000, more

01:36:54.940 --> 01:36:59.090
than its investors could endure. The new league collapsed.

01:37:01.310 --> 01:37:04.390
John Montgomery Ward's brotherhood was crushed,

01:37:05.690 --> 01:37:08.550
and the reserve clause remained firmly in place.

01:37:09.960 --> 01:37:13.950
The players were even more powerless than they had been before.

01:37:16.300 --> 01:37:20.820
Albert Goodwill Spalding's National League swallowed up both its rivals,

01:37:20.920 --> 01:37:28.980
and now, swollen to 12 teams, held a monopoly on Major League Baseball.

01:37:31.020 --> 01:37:34.240
The players' league is deader than the proverbial doornail.

01:37:36.160 --> 01:37:40.600
When the spring comes and the grass is green upon the last resting place of anarchy,

01:37:41.600 --> 01:37:45.080
the national agreement will rise again in all its weight

01:37:46.260 --> 01:37:51.220
and restore to America, in all its purity, its national pastime.

01:37:52.880 --> 01:37:54.760
The great game of baseball.

01:37:56.400 --> 01:37:58.200
Albert Goodwill Spalding.

01:38:17.240 --> 01:38:24.520
The great lesson in sports is supposed to be that you not only learn the elation of winning, but you learn how to lose.

01:38:25.680 --> 01:38:29.980
There's a lot of emphasis on the British attitude towards sports, and Americans have.

01:38:30.320 --> 01:38:36.740
too but there's something very american about being a poor loser refusing to shake the other

01:38:36.740 --> 01:38:41.480
fella's hand he says he's a scoundrel he always was a scoundrel and he's even more than a scoundrel

01:38:41.480 --> 01:38:47.460
now that he's beat me and there's something uh likable about that in people it's bad sportsmanship

01:38:58.050 --> 01:38:59.820
ball was mighty glamorous and

01:39:00.000 --> 01:39:06.620
exciting to me, but there's no use in blinking at the fact that at that time, the game was

01:39:06.620 --> 01:39:12.970
thought by solid, respectable people to be only one degree above grand larceny, arson,

01:39:13.030 --> 01:39:20.390
and mayhem, and those who engaged in it were beneath the notice of decent society. Connie

01:39:20.390 --> 01:39:28.770
Mack. The ballplayers, they were from the fringe of society. These were not educated men. These

01:39:28.770 --> 01:39:29.610
were not men who would

01:39:30.060 --> 01:39:33.960
who knew how to hold a cup of tea with just two fingers and stick out three.

01:39:34.220 --> 01:39:37.660
And the kind of game that they played suited the kind of people that they were.

01:39:40.560 --> 01:39:45.700
Two teams dominated the 90s, the Boston Bean Eaters and the Baltimore Orioles.

01:39:47.000 --> 01:39:52.540
Boston, led by Billy Hamilton and Hugh Duffy, pioneered what would be called the inside game.

01:39:52.990 --> 01:39:55.030
But the Orioles perfected it.

01:39:55.690 --> 01:39:58.770
Sacrifice bunts, squeeze plays, double steals.

01:39:59.350 --> 01:39:59.850
They fought.

01:40:00.000 --> 01:40:01.740
and struggled for every run.

01:40:04.000 --> 01:40:06.500
They were mean, vicious, ready at any time

01:40:06.500 --> 01:40:08.840
to maim a rival player or an umpire

01:40:08.840 --> 01:40:10.000
if it helped their cause.

01:40:11.020 --> 01:40:13.960
Things they would say to an umpire were unbelievably vile.

01:40:14.760 --> 01:40:17.240
They broke the spirits of some very fine men.

01:40:18.600 --> 01:40:20.980
I've seen umpires bathe their feet by the hour

01:40:21.000 --> 01:40:23.000
after they spiked them through their shoes.

01:40:24.550 --> 01:40:26.590
In an era of dirty baseball,

01:40:27.130 --> 01:40:29.750
the Orioles delighted in being the dirtiest.

01:40:31.150 --> 01:40:34.940
Managed by the outfielder Ned Hanlon, known as Foxy Ned,

01:40:35.670 --> 01:40:38.630
the Orioles were one of the greatest teams ever assembled.

01:40:40.160 --> 01:40:42.860
Dan Bruthers, the broad-shouldered first baseman,

01:40:43.060 --> 01:40:45.920
was the greatest power hitter of the 1880s,

01:40:46.140 --> 01:40:48.740
bettering 300 in 14 seasons.

01:40:50.700 --> 01:40:54.780
Wee Willie Keeler in right field was the game's preeminent place hitter.

01:40:55.770 --> 01:40:58.350
Asked for the secret of his success, he answered,

01:40:58.550 --> 01:40:59.670
keep your eye clear.

01:41:00.030 --> 01:41:01.370
and hit them where they ain't.

01:41:02.270 --> 01:41:06.670
He once managed at least one hit in 44 consecutive games.

01:41:08.190 --> 01:41:10.790
The shortstop was Huey Jennings, known

01:41:10.790 --> 01:41:13.130
for his distinctive yell as E-Yah.

01:41:13.730 --> 01:41:18.290
In 1896, he hit 4-0-1, stole 70 bases,

01:41:18.910 --> 01:41:20.870
and set a record in his specialty.

01:41:20.910 --> 01:41:25.650
He managed to get hit by pitched balls 49 times.

01:41:26.740 --> 01:41:29.160
Between seasons, he practiced long.

01:41:31.760 --> 01:41:34.220
They were the ones who devised the famous Baltimore chop.

01:41:35.650 --> 01:41:37.430
They would intentionally hit down on the ball,

01:41:37.700 --> 01:41:40.240
hoping to get a large bounce, which would give the runner time

01:41:40.240 --> 01:41:42.160
to make it all the way to first base

01:41:42.160 --> 01:41:44.540
before the shortstop or second baseman could even field it.

01:41:45.190 --> 01:41:48.890
They would, opposing teams to combat this,

01:41:49.030 --> 01:41:51.470
would flood the area in front of home plate

01:41:51.470 --> 01:41:53.270
so that when they hit down for the Baltimore chop,

01:41:53.410 --> 01:41:54.670
the ball would just stop there,

01:41:54.790 --> 01:41:56.390
the catcher would pick it up and throw the guy out at first.

01:41:57.290 --> 01:41:59.650
It was an entire set of strategies, tactics.

01:42:00.030 --> 01:42:03.350
and most importantly, attitudes, which I think that story reveals,

01:42:04.070 --> 01:42:06.270
that really defined baseball at the time.

01:42:13.860 --> 01:42:17.910
Toughest of the toughs and an abomination of the diamond.

01:42:19.010 --> 01:42:23.860
A rough, unruly man, he uses every low and contemptible method

01:42:23.860 --> 01:42:28.570
that his erratic brain can conceive to win a play by a dirty trick.

01:42:31.240 --> 01:42:34.880
He was, in George Bernard Shaw's words, the one true American.

01:42:34.880 --> 01:42:36.960
When Shaw came to this country and he met McGraw, he said,

01:42:37.100 --> 01:42:38.940
this is it, this is what America's about.

01:42:39.140 --> 01:42:41.900
He was a man who controlled his own destiny

01:42:41.900 --> 01:42:45.220
and attempted to control the destiny of anybody who came near him.

01:42:46.880 --> 01:42:49.320
The most pugnacious Oriole of them all

01:42:49.320 --> 01:42:52.990
was the third baseman, John Joseph McGraw.

01:42:54.640 --> 01:42:56.960
McGraw was born in Truxton, New York,

01:42:57.420 --> 01:42:59.880
the first of eight children of an Irish immigrant.

01:43:00.000 --> 01:43:07.700
railroad worker and his wife. He was a slight, eager 11-year-old whose proudest possession was

01:43:07.700 --> 01:43:13.810
his battered baseball, ordered from the Spalding catalog, when diphtheria struck his village in

01:43:13.810 --> 01:43:22.490
1884. One by one, he watched as first his mother and then four of his brothers and sisters died.

01:43:23.500 --> 01:43:29.670
His father took out his grief and anger on his son, beating him so often and so mercilessly.

01:43:30.250 --> 01:43:33.690
that at 12, he feared for his life and ran away from home.

01:43:35.200 --> 01:43:41.690
He supported himself at odd jobs until he won himself a place

01:43:41.690 --> 01:43:45.370
on the Olean New York professional team at 16

01:43:45.370 --> 01:43:49.900
and never again willingly took orders from any man.

01:43:52.580 --> 01:43:55.440
Young McGraw at third base was a tower of strength,

01:43:55.640 --> 01:43:58.900
but he spoiled his good work by acting like a rowdy.

01:44:00.000 --> 01:44:02.800
Too hot-tempered to be allowed around without a guardian.

01:44:03.970 --> 01:44:08.430
These tough mugs who want to fight on all occasions should be chased out of the business.

01:44:11.600 --> 01:44:18.540
He batted .321 or better over nine consecutive seasons and stole 436 bases.

01:44:20.060 --> 01:44:29.940
Although he was short and weighed barely 120 pounds, he tripped opposing runners on the base paths, blocked them, spiked them, and rarely complained.

01:44:30.000 --> 01:44:37.480
when they did the same to him we'd spit tobacco juice on a spike wound he remembered rub dirt in

01:44:37.480 --> 01:44:46.640
it and get out there and play mayhem seemed to follow john mcgraw wherever he went when he

01:44:46.640 --> 01:44:52.920
got into a fist fight with the opposing third baseman in boston in 1894 both benches empty

01:44:53.140 --> 01:44:59.870
fans began brawling someone set the stands on fire and the entire wooden

01:45:00.030 --> 01:45:14.020
ballpark and 170 neighborhood buildings went up in flames. John McGraw would stay in baseball for

01:45:14.020 --> 01:45:21.520
more than 40 years and become one of the game's greatest managers. But he never stopped fighting,

01:45:21.940 --> 01:45:28.050
never stopped savaging umpires, and was always willing to do just about anything to win.

01:45:31.560 --> 01:45:39.560
The Orioles' combination of trickery, ferocity, and skill won them three National League pennants during the 1890s.

01:45:42.010 --> 01:45:44.790
Rivals, the Boston Bean Eaters, took five.

01:45:47.430 --> 01:45:53.990
Baltimore and Boston were wildly successful, but the two teams so overwhelmed their competition

01:45:53.990 --> 01:45:59.970
that baseball crowds dwindled dangerously for those clubs in other cities that never seemed to rise.

01:46:00.000 --> 01:46:06.530
above 11th or 12th place many fans the long season one of the game's great

01:46:06.530 --> 01:46:14.580
strengths now seemed pointless then a national depression cut further into

01:46:14.580 --> 01:46:20.920
profits and the owners slashed players salaries clergymen and the newspapers

01:46:20.920 --> 01:46:25.960
denounced the rowdyism and scandal that followed the game everywhere and the

01:46:25.960 --> 01:46:28.920
owners seemed incapable of doing anything

01:46:30.960 --> 01:46:35.360
By the end of the 19th century, the professional game was in trouble.

01:46:50.470 --> 01:46:56.150
Laborers leave the shade and quiet of a shop for the sun and fury of a ball ground.

01:46:57.410 --> 01:46:59.970
They stand and they exercise for hours.

01:47:00.000 --> 01:47:05.180
They attest that they mean to be men and not machines.

01:47:10.880 --> 01:47:14.420
Athletic games carry men back to their days of childhood.

01:47:17.000 --> 01:47:24.550
There is indeed morally a home base in all of them, as there is literally in baseball.

01:47:30.000 --> 01:47:34.480
My grandfather was a sour old man, and I liked him. He lived with us.

01:47:36.030 --> 01:47:39.300
But he was out in the backyard throwing a ball when I was a little kid.

01:47:39.960 --> 01:47:41.500
He was smoking his pipe, and he said,

01:47:42.280 --> 01:47:44.330
Do you like baseball? And I said,

01:47:44.550 --> 01:47:45.790
Yeah, yeah. He said, Why do you play?

01:47:46.150 --> 01:47:48.300
I said, I'm a shortstop. And he said,

01:47:48.560 --> 01:47:50.460
That's what I used to play. And I said,

01:47:50.560 --> 01:47:56.580
You used to play? About, oh, a year or so later,

01:47:56.680 --> 01:47:59.560
I found a local paper that had a 50-year anniversary thing,

01:47:59.600 --> 01:47:59.960
and there was a...

01:48:00.000 --> 01:48:04.860
reprint of a game that he had played in in 1892 and it said, Watts, his name was Fred Watts,

01:48:04.940 --> 01:48:13.160
shortstop, and he had two hits. Manfather played baseball in 1892. So it's part of your existence,

01:48:13.380 --> 01:48:21.750
it's part of our heritage. There's a very peaceful thing. It was created and played in pastures and

01:48:21.750 --> 01:48:28.600
meadows. There's grass, there's outdoors, there's everything that people thought was American and

01:48:28.600 --> 01:48:29.720
feel about America.

01:48:30.000 --> 01:48:34.580
get in the ballpark, and it's the wonder of holding your dad's hand, walking through that

01:48:34.580 --> 01:48:41.540
dark tunnel, and seeing a huge open space where men play the little boy's game.

01:49:18.110 --> 01:49:25.640
think about it what is there in your life besides your love of family maybe that

01:49:26.560 --> 01:49:29.880
carries all the way through from almost your earliest recollections

01:49:30.000 --> 01:49:34.050
till the day you die, and you care about it in one way or another.

01:49:34.230 --> 01:49:36.830
There are very, very few things that make that list.

01:49:37.050 --> 01:49:40.340
We come to things at various times in our lives,

01:49:40.360 --> 01:49:42.700
when we can comprehend them, when we have an interest in them.

01:49:42.920 --> 01:49:46.320
But we have a child's interest in baseball.

01:49:46.320 --> 01:49:48.120
In my case, from the time I was five,

01:49:48.850 --> 01:49:51.450
I can't imagine never having an interest in baseball.

01:49:51.650 --> 01:49:53.870
So it will be one of the few things in my life

01:49:53.870 --> 01:49:58.130
that I have cared about in one way or another, all the way through.

01:50:01.950 --> 01:50:08.110
By 1900, Walt Whitman and Alexander Joy Cartwright and Harry Wright had died.

01:50:12.900 --> 01:50:19.680
Baseball had grown from a children's game to a brawling pastime for big city workers to an industry.

01:50:24.500 --> 01:50:29.660
And the names and deeds of its greatest heroes had become familiar in every American home.

01:50:36.720 --> 01:50:42.400
But now, jealousy and greed among the owners, who held a monopoly on Major League Baseball,

01:50:42.910 --> 01:50:45.350
threatened to destroy all that they had built.

01:50:48.910 --> 01:50:53.860
The public was turning to other sports where the amateur spirit had not been lost.

01:50:53.860 --> 01:50:59.190
It would take a new generation of baseball players.

01:51:00.000 --> 01:51:05.040
Stars who would come to represent the best and the worst of the new 20th century

01:51:05.960 --> 01:51:08.400
rescue the national pastime.

01:51:13.090 --> 01:51:20.240
By 1900, Ty Cobb and Casey Stengel and George Herman Ruth had been born.

